  "text": "An Otherworld woman called Étain loved the fairy king Midir. His wife, the druid and sorceress Fuamnach, grew angry at the affair. She cast a spell and turned Étain into a fly. In that form Étain flew around the world for many years. At last she dropped into a cup of wine that an Ulster princess held. The princess drank the insect and became pregnant. Conceiving by drinking an insect is a common Irish motif. The princess bore a girl, named her Étain, and the girl grew into the most beautiful woman in Ireland. Eochaid Airem sought the kingship at Tara. His rites said a king must marry before that day would come. A woman had to play the role of the land goddess. He met Étain in his search, and she had no memory of her old life. She agreed to become his queen. Their wedding and his inauguration went ahead with great ceremony, and the future looked clear. Midir, however, had not left Étain to her peace. He came to Tara in the body of Ailill Anglonnach, the brother of her husband. He said a lack of her touch would cost him his life. Étain touched his hand to save him, though she felt no will for it. In most versions of the story she rejects Midir. Yet he refused to let the matter lie. He returned to Tara in his own body and set a test against Eochaid at the board game Fidchell. Eochaid thought he could win at the play. He had not faced a master of the Otherworld. Midir won the first game. They wagered again, and Eochaid put Étain herself on the stakes. Midir won a second time, and then kissed Étain. The kiss restored her hidden memory of their old love. The pair turned into swans and left Tara behind.",
